</section>

<!-- SELECTING THE RIGHT CONTACT CLEANER FOR YOUR PROJECT -->
<section class="section" id="selecting-the-right-contact-cleaner-for-your-project">
  <h2>Selecting the Right Contact Cleaner for Your Project</h2>
  <p>Selecting the appropriate contact cleaner requires balancing dielectric properties, evaporation rates, and plastic compatibility for the target application. Heavy-duty industrial machinery requires different solvent strengths compared to delicate laboratory test equipment. Formulators customize these parameters through careful raw material selection. The typical selection matrix includes:</p>
  <p>
                        Choosing the right aerosol cleaner involves more than just picking a brand. It requires an
                        analysis of the specific contamination, the sensitivity of the components, and the operational
                        environment. For industrial startups, this selection can impact both the warranty of your
                        products and the safety of your maintenance teams.
                    </p>
                    <h3>Reading the Technical Data Sheet (TDS) and SDS</h3>
                    <p>
                        The TDS is your primary source for dielectric strength, evaporation rate, and KB (Kauri-Butanol)
                        value—a measure of solvent power. A higher KB value indicates a stronger solvent, which is
                        better for heavy grease but potentially more damaging to plastics. Balancing these values is the
                        art of aerosol formulation.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For technical standards on electronics cleaning, visit the <a href="https://www.ipc.org/">IPC - Association Connecting Electronics
                            Industries</a>.
                    </p>
                </section>

